"""
# Imported Optional-Argument Projection Across a Re-export Hop — Regression Spec

Run 15 of the stage-1 hardening lane regressed from 258 to 3716 `[hir-fatal]`
`unresolved type` errors and from 112 to 437 poisoned modules. Every one of the
new names is a type that appears as an OPTIONAL (`X?` == `Option<X>`) or other
GENERIC ARGUMENT in an imported signature/field, reached through a package
facade rather than through the module that declares it:

| name | fatals | shape in the owner |
|---|---|---|
| HirContractBlock | 501 | `verification_contract: HirContractBlock?` (mir_instruction_graph.spl:208) |
| SymbolId | 444 | `owner_symbol: SymbolId?` (module_callable_types.spl:88) |
| Span | 141 | `span: Span?` (mir_instruction_graph.spl:14) |
| LayoutPhase | 84 | `layout_phase: LayoutPhase?` (mir_instruction_graph.spl:177) |

MECHANISM. `imported_surface_projected_named_args` now recurses into the
generic ARGUMENT and calls
`self.symbols.lookup_qualified_type_raw(imported_module_name, arg_name)`.
`imported_module_name` is the module the IMPORTER NAMED -- a package facade or
re-exporter -- not the module that DECLARES the argument type. The lookup
misses, and `imported_surface_type` then falls through to `self.lower_type`,
which resolves in the IMPORTER's scope and emits a HARD, non-recovered
`unresolved type: X` attributed to an innocent module.

Before the generic-argument lane the argument was DROPPED, so the miss was
unreachable. Widening projection did not create the resolution gap; it made an
existing one fatal at scale.

The fix is owner-scope resolution that follows the re-export hop to the
DECLARING module (the same hop `hir_unresolved_type_owner_missing_import` names)
-- NOT restoring the drop, which is the original silent defect.

## THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT YET REPRODUCE THE REGRESSION -- stated, not buried

All three examples below are GREEN at the regressing tree `c05e7052843` AND
green after the revert. That is a NEGATIVE result recorded rather than dressed
up: the four-module facade hop written here is NOT sufficient to trigger the
miss. The real victim, `50.mir/hwir/bit_vector_constant.spl`, has **no `use`
line at all** -- it reaches `MirFunction` through package-sibling auto-import
plus `use compiler.mir.mir_types.*` globs in its siblings, and its profile line
reads `qtype=4725/3781 miss`, an 80% qualified-lookup miss rate that this
fixture does not recreate. Until the fixture goes RED the only reproducer is the
stage-1 census itself (`[hir-fatal]` count 48 -> 3716).

The examples are kept as a NON-VACUOUS guard, not as proof: the third one
pins that the non-generic spelling of the identical type stays clean, so if a
future fix accidentally resolves argument types in the IMPORTER's scope the
first two would pass for the wrong reason and the third would not change.

See doc/08_tracking/bug/hir_unresolved_type_owner_missing_import_2026-08-22.md
and doc/08_tracking/bug/hir_generic_projection_regression_run15_2026-08-22.md
"""

use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.types.{HirLowering, hirlowering_for_module}
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.items.*
use compiler.hir.hir_lowering.module_surface.{ModuleSurfacesByName, module_surfaces_from_modules}
use compiler.hir.hir_types.*
use compiler.frontend.frontend.{parse_full_frontend}
use compiler.frontend.parser_types.{Module}
use compiler.common.driver_core_types.{SourceFile}
use compiler.common.config.{Logger}

fn hop_surfaces(mods: Dict<text, Module>, srcs: [SourceFile]) -> ModuleSurfacesByName:
    match module_surfaces_from_modules(mods, srcs):
        case Ok(value): value
        case Err(error):
            assert_true(false)
            ModuleSurfacesByName.empty()

fn hop_lower(param: text) -> HirLowering:
    """Four modules, mirroring the real tree exactly:

    - `sp.span`      declares `Span`
    - `mir.graph`    IMPORTS `Span` and declares the callable using it
    - `mir`          RE-EXPORTS the callable (the package facade / hop)
    - `mir.hwir.bvc` imports the callable through the FACADE only
    """
    val log = Logger(level: 0)
    val span_src = "struct Span:\n    lo: i64\n"
    val graph_src = "use sp.span.\{Span\}\n\nfn graph_of(" + param + ") -> i64:\n    1\n"
    val facade_src = "export use mir.graph.\{graph_of\}\n"
    val victim_src = "use mir.\{graph_of\}\n\nfn go() -> i64:\n    1\n"
    var mods: Dict<text, Module> = {}
    mods["sp.span"] = parse_full_frontend(span_src, "sp.span", "sp.span", log)
    mods["mir.graph"] = parse_full_frontend(graph_src, "mir.graph", "mir.graph", log)
    mods["mir"] = parse_full_frontend(facade_src, "mir", "mir", log)
    val victim = parse_full_frontend(victim_src, "mir.hwir.bvc", "mir.hwir.bvc", log)
    mods["mir.hwir.bvc"] = victim
    val srcs = [
        SourceFile(path: "sp/span.spl", content: span_src, module_name: "sp.span"),
        SourceFile(path: "mir/graph.spl", content: graph_src, module_name: "mir.graph"),
        SourceFile(path: "mir/__init__.spl", content: facade_src, module_name: "mir"),
        SourceFile(path: "mir/hwir/bvc.spl", content: victim_src, module_name: "mir.hwir.bvc")
    ]
    var lowering = hirlowering_for_module("mir.hwir.bvc", hop_surfaces(mods, srcs))
    val hir = lowering.lower_module(victim)
    lowering

fn hop_unresolved_span_errors(lowering: HirLowering) -> i64:
    var n = 0
    for error in lowering.errors:
        if error.message.contains("unresolved type: Span"):
            n = n + 1
    n

describe "optional generic argument projected across a re-export hop":
    it "does not blame the importer for a type only the OWNER imports":
        # RED at run15's tree: the projected `Option<Span>` argument is looked
        # up in the facade `mir`, misses, falls to the importer's scope, and
        # reports `unresolved type: Span` against mir.hwir.bvc -- a module that
        # never names Span. This is the shape of all 3716 run15 fatals.
        val lowering = hop_lower("s: Span?")
        for error in lowering.errors:
            eprint("[hop-optional-error] {error.message}")
        expect(hop_unresolved_span_errors(lowering)).to_equal(0)

    it "does not blame the importer for a Dict VALUE the owner imports":
        # Same miss, different generic constructor -- the argument recursion is
        # shared, so both branches must clear together.
        val lowering = hop_lower("d: Dict<text, Span>")
        for error in lowering.errors:
            eprint("[hop-dict-error] {error.message}")
        expect(hop_unresolved_span_errors(lowering)).to_equal(0)

    it "guards the guard: a NON-generic use of the same type is unaffected":
        # The bare `s: Span` shape never went through the argument recursion,
        # so it must be green on BOTH sides of the regression. If this ever
        # goes red the fixture has stopped isolating the argument path.
        val lowering = hop_lower("s: Span")
        expect(hop_unresolved_span_errors(lowering)).to_equal(0)
